lonic compounds are more soluble in water or in aqueous medium.
lonic character $\propto$ size of cation (if anion is same)
The order of size of cation is

\mathrm{Na}^{+}>\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}>\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}

$\therefore$ The order of ionic character and hence, of solubility in water is as

\mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{~S}>\mathrm{ZnS}>\mathrm{CuS}

$\mathrm{SnO}_2$ is an amphoteric oxide because it reacts with acids as well as bases to form corresponding salts.

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{SnO}_2+4 \mathrm{HCl} \longrightarrow \mathrm{SnCl}_4+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
& \mathrm{SnO}_2+2 \mathrm{NaOH} \longrightarrow \begin{array}{c}
\mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{SnO}_3+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
\text { sod. stannate }
\end{array}
\end{aligned}

With increase in the number of electrons in the same shell, the atomic radii decreases due to increase in effective nuclear charge. However, atomic radii increases, as the number of shells increases. Thus, on moving down a group atomic radii increases.

The electronic configuration of the given elements is

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Mg}_{12} & =[\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \mathrm{~s}^2 \\
\mathrm{Ca}_{20} & =[\mathrm{Ar}] 4 \mathrm{~s}^2
\end{aligned}

\begin{array}{r}
\mathrm{P}_{15}=[\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \mathrm{~s}^2 3 \mathrm{p}^3 \\
\mathrm{Cl}_{17}=[\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \mathrm{~s}^2 3 \mathrm{p}^5
\end{array}

In $\mathrm{Mg}, \mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}$, the number of electrons are increasing in the same shell, thus the order of their atomic radii is

\mathrm{Cl} < \mathrm{P} < \mathrm{Mg}

In $\mathrm{Ca}$, the electron is entering in higher shell, thus, it has the highest atomic radii among the given. Thus, the order of radii is

\mathrm{Cl} < \mathrm{P} < \mathrm{Mg} < \mathrm{Ca}

Key Idea Stability of lower oxidation states increases on moving down a group due to inert pair effect.
The given elements belong to third group. These elements mainly exhibit +3 and +1 oxidation state. As we know the stability of lower oxidation state, $i e,+1$ state, increases on moving down a group, the sequence of stability is

\mathrm{Al} < \mathrm{Ga} < \mathrm{In} < \mathrm{Tl}

It is known that the value of $E^{\circ}$ becomes more negative down the series. As the chromium is present below in electrochemical series than $\mathrm{Mn}$, so $\mathrm{Mn}$ has more positive $E^{\circ}$ value than $\mathrm{Cr}$.

\begin{array}{ll}
\mathrm{Mn} \rightarrow 3 d^5 4 s^2, & \mathrm{Mn}^{2+} \rightarrow 3 d^5 4 s^0 \\
\mathrm{Cr} \rightarrow 3 d^5 4 s^1, & \mathrm{Cr}^{2+} \rightarrow 3 d^4 4 s^0
\end{array}

For elimination of one electron from $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$, more energy is required due to its stable configuration. That is why third ionization energy of $\mathrm{Mn}$ is larger than $\mathrm{Cr}$.
